<Base title="About Labs" description="Why Labs exists, how its interactive models are built and checked, and what they deliberately leave out.">
  <h1>About Labs</h1>
  <p class="lede">Labs is a place for precise, friendly, evidence-led interactive science, made by Ryan Laird, a science communicator and marketing professional with a physics background.</p>
  <h2>Purpose</h2>
  <p>To help curious readers, students, teachers and communicators build intuition by experimenting, without hiding the maths or overstating what a model can do.</p>
  <h2>Approach</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Model, simulation, measurement.</strong> A model is an equation-based simplification; a simulation runs a model; a measurement is real data. Labs says which is which.</li>
    <li><strong>Equations with units.</strong> Every variable is defined.</li>
    <li><strong>Tested code.</strong> Core calculations have unit tests.</li>
    <li><strong>Accessible and light.</strong> Semantic HTML, keyboard control, reduced-motion support, and minimal JavaScript with no third-party scripts.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <p>Labs models are classroom-scale. They use representative values and simplified physics, and do not reproduce real Earth conditions exactly. They are not forecasting, warning or safety tools. For real-time information, use official agencies.</p>
  <h2>Corrections and ideas</h2>
  <p>Spotted an error? Please <a href="/contact">tell me</a>. Corrections are welcome and will be credited if you wish.</p>
</Base>
